2026-06-30 15:18:12,610 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-9d0f561a-b780-4595-9aca-e0c523de91f2', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nA1_v2_J100_NAQ448_0630_1.raw\nA1_v2_J100_NAQ448_0630_2.raw\nA1_v2_J100_NAQ448_0630_3.raw\nCH146_B1_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nCH146_B2_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nCH146_B3_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nCH146_D1_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nCH146_D2_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nCH146_D3_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nIS20_NAQ448_J100_200305_1.raw\nIS20_NAQ448_J100_200305_2_re.raw\nIS20_NAQ448_J100_200305_3_re.raw\nIS20_NAQ448_J100_200305_4_re.raw\nIssac_45_H_NAQ444_J100_190419.raw\nIssac_45_S_NAQ444_J100_190419.raw\nIssac_45_TA_NAQ444_J100_190419.raw\nIssac_H_WT_02_NAQ448_J100_190718_2.raw\nIssac_H_WT_03_NAQ448_J100_190718_3.raw\nIssac_Mouse22_H_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse22_S_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse22_TA_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse24_H_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse24_S_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse24_TA_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse35_H_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse35_S_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_Mouse35_TA_NAQ448_J100_191101.raw\nIssac_S_WT_02_NAQ448_J100_190718_2.raw\nIssac_S_WT_03_NAQ448_J100_190718_3.raw\nIssac_TA_02_NAQ448_J100_190718.raw\nIssac_TA_03_NAQ448_J100_190718.raw\nNES_LSY_NAQ448_J100_190429_1.raw\nNES_LSY_NAQ448_J100_190429_2.raw\nNES_LSY_NAQ448_J100_190429_3.raw\nTN1_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nTN2_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\nTN3_J100_NAQ_20181127.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nTissue-specific in vivo mitochondrial matrix proteome profiling in mouse\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTargeting proximity labeling enzymes to specific cellular locations is a viable strategy for profiling subcellular proteomes with minimal perturbance to normal physiology. Here, we generate transgenic mice expressing a mitochondrial matrix-targeted ascorbate peroxidase (MTS-APEX2) to enable analysis of tissue-specific matrix proteomes. Desthiobiotin-phenol labeling of the mitochondrial proteome revealed 263 mitochondrial-localized proteins in mouse muscle tissues. Comparison with the MTS-APEX2-labeled proteome in HEK293T cells revealed enrichment in mitochondrial proteins related to energy production in the muscle tissues of transgenic mice. We found Reticulon 4 Interacting Protein 1(RTN4IP1) or Optic Atrophhy-10 (OPA10) was highly enriched in cardiac and soleus muscle and contained a strong mitochondrial matrix-targeting sequence. Structural analysis shows RTN4IP1 is an NADPH oxidoreductase with protein structure resembling quinone oxidoreductase (QOR) in bacterial species. Enzymatic activity assays, interactome analysis, and metabolite profiling confirmed that RTN4IP1 functions in coenzyme Q biosynthesis in the mitochondria. Due to reduced CoQ9 levels, Rtn4ip1-knockout C2C12 cells were vulnerable to oxidative stress and had decreased oxygen consumption rates and ATP production.\n\n[METHODS]\nPeptide sample preparation and enrichment of biotinylated peptides. All prepared in vivo biotinylated plasma samples were first subjected to buffer exchange with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) buffer to completely remove residual free biotin via 10k MWCO filtration for three times. The biotin depleted plasma samples were transferred and denatured with 500 µL of 8 M urea in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABC) for 1 hour at 37°C, and followed by reduction of disulfide bonds with 10 mM dithiothreitol for 1 hour at 37°C. The reduced thiol groups in the protein samples were subsequently alkylated with 40 mM iodoacetamide for 1 hour at 37°C in the dark. The resulting alkylated samples were diluted eight times using 50 mM ABC and subjected to trypsinization at 2% (w/w) trypsin concentration under 1 mM CaCl2 concentration for overnight in Thermomixer (37°C and 500 rpm). Samples were centrifuged at 10,000 × g for 3 min to remove insoluble material. Then, 150 µL of streptavidin beads (Pierce) per replicate was washed with 2 M urea in 1× TBS for four times and combined with the individual digested sample. The combined samples were rotated for 1 hour at room temperature. The flow-through fraction was kept, and the beads were washed twice with 2 M urea in 50 mM ABC and finally with pure water in new tubes. The bound biotinylated peptides were eluted with 400 µL of 80% acetonitrile containing 0.2% TFA and 0.1% formic acid after mixing and heating the beads slurry at 60°C. Each eluate was collected into a new tube. Repeat the elution process four more times. Combined elution fractions were dried using Speed vac (Eppendorf) and reconstituted with 10 µL of 25 mM ABC for further LC-MS/MS experiment.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human); Mus musculus (mouse)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
