2026-06-30 15:18:47,870 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5baba264-a61d-48dc-8c6e-6a9c22a357b1', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nTissue-specific in vivo mitochondrial matrix proteome profiling in mouse\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTargeting proximity labeling enzymes to specific cellular locations is a viable strategy for profiling subcellular proteomes with minimal perturbance to normal physiology. Here, we generate transgenic mice expressing a mitochondrial matrix-targeted ascorbate peroxidase (MTS-APEX2) to enable analysis of tissue-specific matrix proteomes. Desthiobiotin-phenol labeling of the mitochondrial proteome revealed 263 mitochondrial-localized proteins in mouse muscle tissues. Comparison with the MTS-APEX2-labeled proteome in HEK293T cells revealed enrichment in mitochondrial proteins related to energy production in the muscle tissues of transgenic mice. We found Reticulon 4 Interacting Protein 1(RTN4IP1) or Optic Atrophhy-10 (OPA10) was highly enriched in cardiac and soleus muscle and contained a strong mitochondrial matrix-targeting sequence. Structural analysis shows RTN4IP1 is an NADPH oxidoreductase with protein structure resembling quinone oxidoreductase (QOR) in bacterial species. Enzymatic activity assays, interactome analysis, and metabolite profiling confirmed that RTN4IP1 functions in coenzyme Q biosynthesis in the mitochondria. Due to reduced CoQ9 levels, Rtn4ip1-knockout C2C12 cells were vulnerable to oxidative stress and had decreased oxygen consumption rates and ATP production.\n\n[METHODS]\nPeptide sample preparation and enrichment of biotinylated peptides. All prepared in vivo biotinylated plasma samples were first subjected to buffer exchange with phosphate-buffered saline (PBS) buffer to completely remove residual free biotin via 10k MWCO filtration for three times. The biotin depleted plasma samples were transferred and denatured with 500 µL of 8 M urea in 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (ABC) for 1 hour at 37°C, and followed by reduction of disulfide bonds with 10 mM dithiothreitol for 1 hour at 37°C. The reduced thiol groups in the protein samples were subsequently alkylated with 40 mM iodoacetamide for 1 hour at 37°C in the dark. The resulting alkylated samples were diluted eight times using 50 mM ABC and subjected to trypsinization at 2% (w/w) trypsin concentration under 1 mM CaCl2 concentration for overnight in Thermomixer (37°C and 500 rpm). Samples were centrifuged at 10,000 × g for 3 min to remove insoluble material. Then, 150 µL of streptavidin beads (Pierce) per replicate was washed with 2 M urea in 1× TBS for four times and combined with the individual digested sample. The combined samples were rotated for 1 hour at room temperature. The flow-through fraction was kept, and the beads were washed twice with 2 M urea in 50 mM ABC and finally with pure water in new tubes. The bound biotinylated peptides were eluted with 400 µL of 80% acetonitrile containing 0.2% TFA and 0.1% formic acid after mixing and heating the beads slurry at 60°C. Each eluate was collected into a new tube. Repeat the elution process four more times. Combined elution fractions were dried using Speed vac (Eppendorf) and reconstituted with 10 µL of 25 mM ABC for further LC-MS/MS experiment.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
